================================================================================================
(2)  RESAMPLING ALONE:  ratio with the readout variance SHARED
================================================================================================
    Sp    sum Var V5   sum Var SPSA    ratio
  ------------------------------------------
     1       0.02411        0.03778     1.57
     2       0.02346        0.06341     2.70
     4       0.02350        0.12019     5.11
     8       0.02410        0.22832     9.47

  SPSA would choose the Sp that minimises its own variance, so the honest
  comparison is against the best row. If that best row sits near 2, then
  resampling alone does NOT explain 5.66x.

================================================================================================
(3)  UNSHARED READOUT:  V5 bounded at v<=1, SPSA paying Var(H_g)
================================================================================================
  V5's shot is a bounded +-1 ancilla bit. SPSA's shot is an energy estimate
  whose variance is Var(H_g) and is not bounded by 1.

    Var(H_g)   Sp*    sum Var V5   sum Var SPSA    ratio
  ------------------------------------------------------
         1.0     1       0.02418        0.03802     1.57
         4.0     1       0.02317        0.06433     2.78
        16.0     1       0.02373        0.17667     7.44
        64.0     1       0.02332        0.60469    25.93

  Reaching ~5x here, at a Var(H_g) the tested Hamiltonians actually have,
  would locate v72's margin in the readout rather than in the superposition
  and would agree with v67, which found the superposition alone buys nothing.
  Failing to reach it for any plausible Var(H_g) would mean this model does
  not contain the effect v72 measured, which is worth more than either.
